---
import DiagramFigure from './DiagramFigure.astro'

// Mutation testing, read for BLAST RADIUS rather than for survivors.
//
// Atlas specifies its suite as a kill list of 31 named mutations, referenced by id in the test
// docstrings themselves (see the "Kill list rows covered: M01 (render truth), M02 (scope), ..."
// header in tests/test_guard.py). All 31 of 31 turn the suite red, so there are ZERO survivors and
// the kill rate carries no information at all. An earlier draft of this figure showed three
// surviving mutants in a retrieval metric suite. Those three survivors were never measured in this
// repo, and inventing survivors to make the point would be the exact failure this series is about.
//
// The information is in how MANY tests each mutation turns red, across 573 tests in 15 files:
//   min 1 test (0.17%), median 5 (0.87%), max 27 (4.71%)
// And the finding worth the whole exercise is M01. Disabling the cold open truth check now fires 27
// tests across three files. In the OLD tree, that same mutation left test_guard.py, the file named
// after the guard, completely green: eight tests, zero coverage of the rule the project exists to
// demonstrate. Reading the suite never found that. Mutating it did.

const rows = [
  {
    id: 'M01',
    mutation: 'disable the cold open truth check',
    radius: '27 tests · 3 files · 4.71%',
    tone: 'wide' as const,
    found:
      'in the old tree this same mutation left test_guard.py entirely green: eight tests, none of them covering the rule the project exists to demonstrate.',
  },
  {
    id: 'median',
    mutation: 'the typical row on the kill list',
    radius: '5 tests · 0.87%',
    tone: 'mid' as const,
    found:
      'a handful of tests per mutation is healthy: enough that a real regression is loud, few enough that the suite is not saying the same thing twelve times.',
  },
  {
    id: 'min',
    mutation: 'the thinnest row on the kill list',
    radius: '1 test · 0.17%',
    tone: 'thin' as const,
    found:
      'exactly one test stands between this behaviour and silence. Delete it in a tidy-up and the mutation starts surviving, with the suite still green.',
  },
]

const ariaLabel =
  'Mutation testing read as blast radius across a suite of 573 tests in 15 files. All 31 mutations on the kill list turn the suite red, so there are no survivors and the kill rate says nothing. What varies is how many tests each mutation fires. Mutation M01, disabling the cold open truth check, fires 27 tests across three files, 4.71% of the suite; in the old tree that same mutation left the file named after the guard completely green, eight tests with no coverage of the rule the project exists to demonstrate. The median mutation fires five tests, 0.87%. The thinnest fires a single test, 0.17%, meaning one deletion away from surviving unnoticed.'
---

<DiagramFigure label="Mutation testing: read the blast radius, not the kill rate" ariaLabel={ariaLabel} data-ms>
  <div class="ms-head rounded border border-border-dark bg-surface-container-low px-4 py-3 mb-3">
    <span class="block font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-text-muted">the headline that tells you nothing</span>
    <span class="block text-sm text-text-primary mt-1 leading-snug">
      31 of 31 mutations turn the suite red. Zero survivors, a 100% kill rate, and no information in it.
    </span>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-3">
    {
      rows.map((r) => (
        <div class="ms-row rounded border border-border-dark bg-surface-container-low overflow-hidden">
          <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 border-b border-border-dark">
            <code class="font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-primary">{r.id}</code>
            <span class="font-mono text-mono-xs text-text-muted">{r.mutation}</span>
            <span class:list={['ms-radius', `ms-${r.tone}`, 'font-mono text-mono-xs px-2 py-0.5 sm:ml-auto']}>{r.radius}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-surface px-4 py-3">
            <span class="block font-mono text-mono-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-text-muted">what the radius tells you</span>
            <span class="block text-sm text-text-primary mt-1 leading-snug">{r.found}</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="note">
    A kill rate of 100% is the least interesting thing a mutation run can report, because it is what
    you get whether the suite is thorough or merely broad. The spread is the finding. One mutation
    firing 27 tests and another firing exactly one, in the same suite, tells you which behaviours are
    genuinely defended and which are held up by a single assertion nobody would miss. Mutation
    testing did not grade this suite. It told us where to look.
  </Fragment>
</DiagramFigure>

<style is:global>
  [data-ms] .ms-radius {
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  [data-ms] .ms-wide {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 40%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 8%, transparent);
  }
  [data-ms] .ms-mid {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border-dark);
  }
  [data-ms] .ms-thin {
    color: var(--color-error);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-error) 40%, transparent);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-error) 8%, transparent);
  }
</style>
